          "heading": "Theoretical Overview: Geometric Representation of Signals, Gram-Schmidt Orthogonalization, and Vector AWGN",
          "content": "# Unit 1 \u2014 Error detection and classical channel codes\n\n\n\n## Why this unit exists\nThe unit centers on **reliable industrial telemetry**, where a factory sends short vibration alerts from sensors to a wired gateway. Its controlling difficulty is that a false negative could hide a developing fault while a false positive could halt a production line. The four lessons isolate different parts of the mechanism, yet the delivered system must satisfy all of them at once. You will link equations to implementation, design measurements and deliberately test situations in which a convenient idealization becomes misleading.\n\n## Concept sequence\n\n### Lesson 1.1 \u2014 Framing, errors and CRC detection\n\nA packet is not useful merely because its waveform is recognizable. It must have defined boundaries, an error detection policy and a receiver rule that prevents corrupted application data from being accepted. A CRC is typically a detection mechanism, not a replacement for a forward-error-correcting decoder. Burst errors whose polynomial has degree smaller than the generator degree are detected when the generator has a nonzero leading and constant term. This guarantee is about bursts, not about arbitrary patterns of the same weight.\n\n**Key relation:** A CRC performs polynomial division of a bitstream over GF(2). The transmitted block is a multiple of generator g(x); a nonzero received remainder flags a detectable error pattern.\n\n**Design boundary:** A zero syndrome only establishes compatibility with the CRC constraint, not that the channel introduced no errors. The popular 2^-r undetected-error approximation assumes a sufficiently random error model and should not be applied mechanically to adversarial patterns.\n\n\n\n### Lesson 1.2 \u2014 Linear block codes and parity-check matrices\n\nA systematic generator matrix G can encode information symbols directly into the first k positions followed by check bits when a suitable column ordering exists. Redundancy reduces code rate R=k/n but can increase net reliability at a fixed transmitted energy budget. The minimum Hamming distance of a code controls guaranteed correction and detection. A distance d_min code corrects up to floor((d_min-1)/2) errors in minimum-distance decoding under hard decisions.\n\n**Key relation:** For binary linear code C, each valid codeword c satisfies H c^T = 0 (mod 2), and syndrome s = H r^T identifies a coset of candidate errors.\n\n**Design boundary:** Code rate is not itself the energy penalty: Eb must be defined per information bit, whereas Es relates to channel symbols. Comparing codes at fixed symbol energy without accounting for throughput can create a fictional coding gain.\n\n\n\n### Lesson 1.3 \u2014 Cyclic codes and syndrome interpretation\n\nCyclic shifts of a valid cyclic-code word remain valid, which allows compact shift-register implementations. Systematic encoding requires division to form the remainder parity polynomial. A decoder compares a received remainder against expected error patterns; the computational feasibility of more advanced decoding depends on algebraic code structure and the block parameters. A CRC generator is chosen for detection properties rather than because it can uniquely decode every error.\n\n**Key relation:** A cyclic code is an ideal in GF(2)[x]/(x^n-1) and can be generated by a polynomial g(x) dividing x^n-1.\n\n**Design boundary:** Polynomial notation is sensitive to bit-order conventions. Whether the leftmost bit is the highest-degree coefficient must be fixed before comparing a software CRC with a published lookup table.\n\n\n\n### Lesson 1.4 \u2014 Convolutional encoding and Viterbi decoding\n\nConvolutional codes map a stream and encoder memory into a sequence of output symbols. The state is the prior input history. At each trellis stage, path metrics accumulate branch mismatch costs, and survivor paths keep only the most promising predecessor per state. Hard-input decoding uses Hamming distances; soft-input decoding uses channel likelihood metrics and often performs better when calibrated soft observations are available. Tail bits can drive the encoder to a known final state, changing the effective rate for short packets.\n\n**Key relation:** A rate-1/2 memory-2 convolutional code can use generator polynomials (111,101) in binary; the Viterbi algorithm finds the minimum-metric trellis path.\n\n**Design boundary:** A Viterbi decoder is optimal for the chosen trellis and additive metric, not universally optimal when the noise is correlated or the receiver supplies inaccurate soft metrics.\n\n\n\n## Integrated case study\n\nAn engineer reviews the architecture for reliable industrial telemetry. The first review identifies the performance or integrity quantity at the receiver. The second asks which subsystem can change that quantity. The third checks the impact on user payload, energy consumption, delay and operating cost. A favorable conclusion requires comparable conditions; changing both the channel model and signal power while comparing decoders makes it impossible to attribute improvement to one change.\n\n### Three-stage assessment plan\n\n1. **Analytical baseline.** Derive a transparent relation using one of the four listed key models. Describe the allowed parameter region, units and what was held constant in the comparison. For reliable industrial telemetry, decide which model variable is realistically measurable and which must be inferred.\n\n2. **Synthetic controlled test.** Isolate one impairment, vary its magnitude and compare to the baseline. Re-run with a different seed and a second numerical implementation. Build a plot with labeled axes and, for error probabilities, uncertainty intervals.\n\n3. **Engineering hand-off.** Replace the idealized assumption with a more plausible impairment relevant to a factory sends short vibration alerts from sensors to a wired gateway. State which earlier conclusion remains reliable and which needs further data. Record what must be checked before a prototype is trusted outside the laboratory.\n\n### Unit questions\n\n- Which of the four mechanisms is most likely to control acceptance for reliable industrial telemetry, and what measured evidence would establish that claim?\n\n- How would the design fail if the assumption \u201cA zero syndrome only establishes compatibility with the CRC constraint, not that the channel introduced no errors. The popular 2^-r undetected-error approximation assumes a sufficiently random error model and should not be applied mechanically to adversarial patterns.\u201d were ignored?\n\n- Identify a numerical sanity check, an implementation test, and a separate real-world validation requirement.\n\n- Where would additional redundancy help and where might it merely increase latency or occupy already-limited bandwidth?\n\n### Suggested learning order\n\nRead the four lessons in order; complete the simulation, then submit the project with a result table and the associated limitations.\n"
