          "heading": "Analytical Derivations and Assessment: Geometric Representation of Signals, Gram-Schmidt Orthogonalization, and Vector AWGN",
          "content": "# Unit 1 assessment \u2014 Error detection and classical channel codes\n\n**Context:** a factory sends short vibration alerts from sensors to a wired gateway\n\n## Problems (at least three required per unit)\n\n### Question 1: Framing, errors and CRC detection\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **A CRC performs polynomial division of a bitstream over GF(2). The transmitted block is a multiple of generator g(x); a nonzero received remainder flags a detectable error pattern.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: A zero syndrome only establishes compatibility with the CRC constraint, not that the channel introduced no errors. The popular 2^-r undetected-error approximation assumes a sufficiently random error model and should not be applied mechanically to adversarial patterns.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 2: Linear block codes and parity-check matrices\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **For binary linear code C, each valid codeword c satisfies H c^T = 0 (mod 2), and syndrome s = H r^T identifies a coset of candidate errors.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: Code rate is not itself the energy penalty: Eb must be defined per information bit, whereas Es relates to channel symbols. Comparing codes at fixed symbol energy without accounting for throughput can create a fictional coding gain.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 3: Cyclic codes and syndrome interpretation\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **A cyclic code is an ideal in GF(2)[x]/(x^n-1) and can be generated by a polynomial g(x) dividing x^n-1.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: Polynomial notation is sensitive to bit-order conventions. Whether the leftmost bit is the highest-degree coefficient must be fixed before comparing a software CRC with a published lookup table.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 4: Convolutional encoding and Viterbi decoding\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **A rate-1/2 memory-2 convolutional code can use generator polynomials (111,101) in binary; the Viterbi algorithm finds the minimum-metric trellis path.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: A Viterbi decoder is optimal for the chosen trellis and additive metric, not universally optimal when the noise is correlated or the receiver supplies inaccurate soft metrics.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 5: Joint design\n\nCombine two lessons in the reliable industrial telemetry case. Propose a before/after experiment where only one controlled variable changes and show how you will report both a link-level metric and a delivered-payload metric.\n\n**Mark scheme:** prioritize controlled comparison (4), technically justified outcome (4), reproducible evidence (2).\n\n### Question 6: Critical evidence review\n\nFind a plausible but unsupported claim of improvement, identify which additional variable may account for the effect, and design one negative control to disprove your own conclusion.\n\n**Mark scheme:** claims supported by computed or experimentally generated data receive credit; appeals to authority or unsupported statements do not.\n\n## Submission policy\n\nUse explicit parameter values, code or workings, and a conclusion that distinguishes verified arithmetic from engineering assumptions.\n"
