          "content": "# Unit 2 assessment \u2014 Modern coding and probabilistic decoding\n\n**Context:** a remote probe sends diagnostic records through a noisy long-distance link\n\n## Problems (at least three required per unit)\n\n### Question 1: Log-likelihood ratios and soft information\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **For equally likely BPSK symbols x in {+1,-1} over AWGN with noise variance sigma^2, L=log[p(y|+1)/p(y|-1)]=2y/sigma^2.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: The formula assumes memoryless Gaussian noise and correctly scaled observations. Channel mismatch, clipping and non-Gaussian interference can make high-magnitude LLRs misleading.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 2: Sparse parity checks and LDPC iteration\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **For parity-check matrix H, an LDPC code uses sparse constraints Hc=0; belief propagation exchanges variable-to-check and check-to-variable messages on a Tanner graph.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: Short cycles and absorbing sets cause error floors. More iterations can consume time and power without reducing error under poor channel estimates. Never compare LDPC and uncoded BER while silently changing information-bit energy.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 3: Turbo codes and extrinsic-information exchange\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **Turbo decoding iterates between soft-in/soft-out constituent decoders, exchanging extrinsic LLRs through an interleaver.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: Iterative information transfer assumes meaningful extrinsic messages. Short interleavers, correlation between messages, poor termination and quantized arithmetic can harm convergence.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 4: Polar transforms, frozen positions and CRC-aided decisions\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **The kernel F=[[1,0],[1,1]] over GF(2) forms Kronecker-power polarization transforms; selected synthetic channels carry information while others are frozen.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: A code optimized for one channel reliability ordering may perform poorly on another. Finite-length performance cannot be inferred solely from asymptotic capacity arguments.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 5: Joint design\n\nCombine two lessons in the coded low-power spacecraft downlink case. Propose a before/after experiment where only one controlled variable changes and show how you will report both a link-level metric and a delivered-payload metric.\n\n**Mark scheme:** prioritize controlled comparison (4), technically justified outcome (4), reproducible evidence (2).\n\n### Question 6: Critical evidence review\n\nFind a plausible but unsupported claim of improvement, identify which additional variable may account for the effect, and design one negative control to disprove your own conclusion.\n\n**Mark scheme:** claims supported by computed or experimentally generated data receive credit; appeals to authority or unsupported statements do not.\n\n## Submission policy\n\nUse explicit parameter values, code or workings, and a conclusion that distinguishes verified arithmetic from engineering assumptions.\n"
