          "heading": "Analytical Derivations and Assessment: M-ary Passband Modulation: QPSK, M-PSK, M-QAM, and Constellation Geometry",
          "content": "# Unit 3 assessment \u2014 Receiver estimation and equalization\n\n**Context:** a moving receiver crosses an area with multipath echoes and oscillator drift\n\n## Problems (at least three required per unit)\n\n### Question 1: Matched filters and ML/MAP decisions\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **For deterministic signal s in AWGN with covariance N0/2 per real dimension, a matched filter maximizes output sample SNR for a specified sampling instant.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: A receiver can improve total decision accuracy by using unequal priors while becoming less fair to the rare event. State the cost function before calling the threshold 'optimal'.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 2: Channels with memory and ISI equalizers\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **A discrete-time channel y[k]=sum_l h[l]x[k-l]+n[k] creates intersymbol interference; a finite-tap linear equalizer attempts to invert or regularize this convolution.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: A frequency zero within the signaling band makes exact stable inversion problematic. An equalizer cannot restore information lost below the noise floor without paying an SNR penalty.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 3: Pilot-based channel estimates and LMS adaptation\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **With observed y[k]\u2248h x[k]+n[k], a scalar pilot least-squares estimate is h_hat=sum x*[k]y[k] / sum |x[k]|^2.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: A small training residual does not validate generalization across time, carrier frequency or device temperature. Using pilots that are not actually orthogonal can bias a multichannel estimate.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 4: Carrier recovery and phase-locked estimation\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **An oscillator mismatch introduces phase theta[k]=theta0+2*pi*Delta_f*k*T_s, rotating complex baseband symbols as exp(j theta[k]).**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: Arctangent phase detectors wrap at \u00b1\u03c0; direct unwrapping can fail near deep fades. A loop that tracks a noisy data decision may lock to a wrong stable phase.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 5: Joint design\n\nCombine two lessons in the urban wireless receiver case. Propose a before/after experiment where only one controlled variable changes and show how you will report both a link-level metric and a delivered-payload metric.\n\n**Mark scheme:** prioritize controlled comparison (4), technically justified outcome (4), reproducible evidence (2).\n\n### Question 6: Critical evidence review\n\nFind a plausible but unsupported claim of improvement, identify which additional variable may account for the effect, and design one negative control to disprove your own conclusion.\n\n**Mark scheme:** claims supported by computed or experimentally generated data receive credit; appeals to authority or unsupported statements do not.\n\n## Submission policy\n\nUse explicit parameter values, code or workings, and a conclusion that distinguishes verified arithmetic from engineering assumptions.\n"
