          "id": "sec-4-7",
          "secNumber": "4.7",
          "heading": "Analytical Derivations and Assessment: Non-Coherent Detection, Differential Phase Shift Keying (DPSK), and Continuous-Phase FSK",
          "content": "# Unit 4 assessment \u2014 Multicarrier and OFDM systems\n\n**Context:** a local network uses multicarrier transmission between fixed rooftop endpoints\n\n## Problems (at least three required per unit)\n\n### Question 1: DFT subcarriers and useful symbol duration\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **For N-point OFDM over useful duration T_u, subcarrier spacing \u0394f=1/T_u; the time-domain symbol is an inverse DFT of complex subcarrier values.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: Subcarrier orthogonality is destroyed by significant synchronization error, rapid time variation or inadequate guard interval. A finite FFT grid does not guarantee a compliant continuous-time emission mask.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 2: Cyclic prefix, delay spread and equalization\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **Appending a cyclic prefix of L samples to an N-sample OFDM block converts a channel of length \u2264L+1 taps into a circular convolution over the retained N samples when timing is correct.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: A longer cyclic prefix cannot solve very rapid Doppler changes during a single FFT window. It also does not correct phase noise or interference from adjacent transmitters.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 3: Frequency offset and intercarrier interference\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **For normalized carrier offset \u03b5=\u0394f_off/\u0394f_sub, each received OFDM tone leaks energy into other bins; desired-tone magnitude is approximately |sin(\u03c0 \u03b5)/(N sin(\u03c0 \u03b5/N))| for an N-sample rectangular FFT window.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: The sinc approximation is for the rectangular window and idealized single-offset model. Phase noise spreads energy differently and cannot be modeled by replacing it with a single constant frequency error.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 4: PAPR, amplifier clipping and EVM\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **For discrete samples x[n], PAPR=max_n |x[n]|\u00b2 / mean_n |x[n]|\u00b2; clipping a multicarrier waveform adds in-band distortion and out-of-band regrowth.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: An FFT at the symbol sampling grid may miss continuous-time peaks. Compare oversampled PAPR estimates before claiming that a waveform meets amplifier specifications.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 5: Joint design\n\nCombine two lessons in the multicarrier campus backhaul case. Propose a before/after experiment where only one controlled variable changes and show how you will report both a link-level metric and a delivered-payload metric.\n\n**Mark scheme:** prioritize controlled comparison (4), technically justified outcome (4), reproducible evidence (2).\n\n### Question 6: Critical evidence review\n\nFind a plausible but unsupported claim of improvement, identify which additional variable may account for the effect, and design one negative control to disprove your own conclusion.\n\n**Mark scheme:** claims supported by computed or experimentally generated data receive credit; appeals to authority or unsupported statements do not.\n\n## Submission policy\n\nUse explicit parameter values, code or workings, and a conclusion that distinguishes verified arithmetic from engineering assumptions.\n"
