          "id": "sec-5-6",
          "secNumber": "5.6",
          "heading": "Engineering Design Project: Information Measure, Source Entropy, Shannon-Fano, and Huffman Source Coding",
          "content": "# Unit 5 project \u2014 Fading, diversity and spatial communication\n\n## Design brief\nCreate an original computer-based engineering investigation of mobile vehicle-to-infrastructure channel: a moving vehicle exchanges sensor messages while surrounding reflectors change. The stakeholder fears that coverage holes and packet deadlines matter more than average SNR alone. Deliver a short technical report that combines the four models in Unit 5, an independent numeric calculation, a simple synthetic implementation and a comparison between two candidate solutions.\n\n## Required artifact A \u2014 model assumptions and operating points\n\nWrite a system block diagram and a table defining the reference points for all quantities in the four lessons. Include a benign operating point and two stresses, one concerning **time, frequency and spatial diversity** and the other concerning **mimo capacity and rank limitations**. Give symbolic units and numerical values. Make clear which values are synthetic; do not label simulated observations as measured data.\n\n## Required artifact B \u2014 two methods, one controlled comparison\n\nImplement an equation or algorithm from **Rayleigh fading statistics and outage**, then independently recompute a selected point. Next vary an input related to **Space-time block coding and Alamouti structure** while holding the baseline model fixed. Record the dependent metric at at least ten settings; if the output is an estimated failure probability, run multiple seeds and show sampling uncertainty. Report a parameter setting at which the second candidate outperforms the first and one at which it does not.\n\n## Required artifact C \u2014 intentionally broken assumption\n\nThe limitations you must investigate include: (1) Shadow fading, spatial correlation and a line-of-sight component change the distribution. A Rayleigh formula cannot describe all propagation environments or a site-specific coverage map. (2) If the channel changes between time slots, the textbook orthogonality cancellation no longer holds exactly. Antenna power normalization must also be stated when comparing with one-transmit operation. Choose the more relevant limitation for mobile vehicle-to-infrastructure channel, inject an impairment that invalidates the original assumption, and explain in technical language why the ideal calculation no longer predicts the result. An analysis that merely says \u201cnoise got worse\u201d will not pass this requirement.\n\n## Required artifact D \u2014 user-level consequence\n\nThe application is a moving vehicle exchanges sensor messages while surrounding reflectors change. Define payload size, raw resource rate, protocol overhead, retransmission rule and the deadline for an accepted message. Trace low-level failure through framing and decoding into delivery success, delay and useful goodput. Prefer a table containing both one-attempt performance and end-to-end outcomes. Explain what real deployment measurements would be required.\n\n## Reproducibility and reporting rubric\n\nSubmit the source code, version or runtime description, seed list, parameter ranges and generated CSV. Use descriptive filenames, include a short README with instructions for a fresh run, and test one identity or limiting case. Cite the assumptions that make each formula valid. Allocate 30% to physics and mathematical correctness, 25% to experiment design, 20% to interpretation and failure analysis, 15% to reproducibility, and 10% to clarity. An unsupported claim of production readiness receives no credit.\n\n## Oral defense prompts\n\nExplain why **Time, frequency and spatial diversity** was included or excluded in the final design. Which numerical result is most sensitive to a poorly known parameter? What single new piece of hardware or field evidence would most change the conclusion? Finally, explain why the synthetic model is adequate for learning but does not establish a radio license, an optical product certification or safety compliance.\n"
