          "id": "sec-5-7",
          "secNumber": "5.7",
          "heading": "Analytical Derivations and Assessment: Information Measure, Source Entropy, Shannon-Fano, and Huffman Source Coding",
          "content": "# Unit 5 assessment \u2014 Fading, diversity and spatial communication\n\n**Context:** a moving vehicle exchanges sensor messages while surrounding reflectors change\n\n## Problems (at least three required per unit)\n\n### Question 1: Rayleigh fading statistics and outage\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **For normalized Rayleigh flat fading, G=|h|\u00b2 is exponential with mean 1; outage at threshold \u03b3_th with mean SNR \u03b3_bar is P_out=1-exp(-\u03b3_th/\u03b3_bar).**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: Shadow fading, spatial correlation and a line-of-sight component change the distribution. A Rayleigh formula cannot describe all propagation environments or a site-specific coverage map.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 2: Time, frequency and spatial diversity\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **For independent exponentially distributed branch SNRs, selection combining of M equal-mean branches yields P_out=(1-exp(-\u03b3_th/\u03b3_bar))^M.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: Antenna spacing does not guarantee zero correlation, especially with narrow angular spread or nearby coupling. Noise covariance matters when forming optimal linear combiners.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 3: Space-time block coding and Alamouti structure\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **A two-transmit one-receive Alamouti block sends [[s1,s2],[-s2*,s1*]] across two time slots under a channel constant over the block, enabling linear orthogonal combining.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: If the channel changes between time slots, the textbook orthogonality cancellation no longer holds exactly. Antenna power normalization must also be stated when comparing with one-transmit operation.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 4: MIMO capacity and rank limitations\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **For Gaussian MIMO with channel H and receiver noise covariance I, C=log2 det(I+(\u03c1/Nt)HH\u1d34) bits/s/Hz when transmit power is equally allocated over Nt antennas and \u03c1 is total linear SNR.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: Capacity is not the modulation order or an achievable payload rate without coding and overhead. A nearly singular channel can make naive spatial multiplexing extremely error-prone.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 5: Joint design\n\nCombine two lessons in the mobile vehicle-to-infrastructure channel case. Propose a before/after experiment where only one controlled variable changes and show how you will report both a link-level metric and a delivered-payload metric.\n\n**Mark scheme:** prioritize controlled comparison (4), technically justified outcome (4), reproducible evidence (2).\n\n### Question 6: Critical evidence review\n\nFind a plausible but unsupported claim of improvement, identify which additional variable may account for the effect, and design one negative control to disprove your own conclusion.\n\n**Mark scheme:** claims supported by computed or experimentally generated data receive credit; appeals to authority or unsupported statements do not.\n\n## Submission policy\n\nUse explicit parameter values, code or workings, and a conclusion that distinguishes verified arithmetic from engineering assumptions.\n"
