          "heading": "Engineering Design Project: Channel Capacity, Mutual Information, and Shannon Noisy-Channel Coding Theorem",
          "content": "# Unit 6 project \u2014 Multiple access and synchronization\n\n## Design brief\nCreate an original computer-based engineering investigation of multiuser field-monitoring network: many low-power remote stations periodically report data to a shared collector. The stakeholder fears that contention, power imbalance and receiver synchronization dominate crowded periods. Deliver a short technical report that combines the four models in Unit 6, an independent numeric calculation, a simple synthetic implementation and a comparison between two candidate solutions.\n\n## Required artifact A \u2014 model assumptions and operating points\n\nWrite a system block diagram and a table defining the reference points for all quantities in the four lessons. Include a benign operating point and two stresses, one concerning **code division and the near-far problem** and the other concerning **frequency hopping and coexistence**. Give symbolic units and numerical values. Make clear which values are synthetic; do not label simulated observations as measured data.\n\n## Required artifact B \u2014 two methods, one controlled comparison\n\nImplement an equation or algorithm from **Direct-sequence spreading and processing gain**, then independently recompute a selected point. Next vary an input related to **Timing acquisition and synchronization loops** while holding the baseline model fixed. Record the dependent metric at at least ten settings; if the output is an estimated failure probability, run multiple seeds and show sampling uncertainty. Report a parameter setting at which the second candidate outperforms the first and one at which it does not.\n\n## Required artifact C \u2014 intentionally broken assumption\n\nThe limitations you must investigate include: (1) The processing-gain ratio is not automatically a net SNR gain in additive white noise after a matched receiver when transmit energy per bit is held constant. (2) A high correlation peak can occur at a delayed echo; earliest-arrival ranging demands more than choosing the global maximum. Interference and sample-rate mismatch can also shift estimates. Choose the more relevant limitation for multiuser field-monitoring network, inject an impairment that invalidates the original assumption, and explain in technical language why the ideal calculation no longer predicts the result. An analysis that merely says \u201cnoise got worse\u201d will not pass this requirement.\n\n## Required artifact D \u2014 user-level consequence\n\nThe application is many low-power remote stations periodically report data to a shared collector. Define payload size, raw resource rate, protocol overhead, retransmission rule and the deadline for an accepted message. Trace low-level failure through framing and decoding into delivery success, delay and useful goodput. Prefer a table containing both one-attempt performance and end-to-end outcomes. Explain what real deployment measurements would be required.\n\n## Reproducibility and reporting rubric\n\nSubmit the source code, version or runtime description, seed list, parameter ranges and generated CSV. Use descriptive filenames, include a short README with instructions for a fresh run, and test one identity or limiting case. Cite the assumptions that make each formula valid. Allocate 30% to physics and mathematical correctness, 25% to experiment design, 20% to interpretation and failure analysis, 15% to reproducibility, and 10% to clarity. An unsupported claim of production readiness receives no credit.\n\n## Oral defense prompts\n\nExplain why **Code division and the near-far problem** was included or excluded in the final design. Which numerical result is most sensitive to a poorly known parameter? What single new piece of hardware or field evidence would most change the conclusion? Finally, explain why the synthetic model is adequate for learning but does not establish a radio license, an optical product certification or safety compliance.\n"
