          "content": "# Unit 6 assessment \u2014 Multiple access and synchronization\n\n**Context:** many low-power remote stations periodically report data to a shared collector\n\n## Problems (at least three required per unit)\n\n### Question 1: Direct-sequence spreading and processing gain\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **Multiplying a data symbol by an N-chip \u00b11 spreading sequence increases occupied chip rate; nominal processing gain is 10log10(N) dB when chip and symbol periods satisfy Tc=Ts/N.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: The processing-gain ratio is not automatically a net SNR gain in additive white noise after a matched receiver when transmit energy per bit is held constant.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 2: Code division and the near-far problem\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **In a two-user spread-spectrum receiver, the undesired despread amplitude is proportional to sqrt(P_interference) times code cross-correlation; near-far imbalance can dominate small correlation values.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: Power-control error and capture effect interact with receiver dynamic range; a digital multiuser detector cannot recover an ADC signal that has already saturated.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 3: Timing acquisition and synchronization loops\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **For a known preamble p[n], acquisition can maximize |sum_n r[n+m]p*[n]| over candidate delays m; the strongest peak need not equal the earliest propagation path.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: A high correlation peak can occur at a delayed echo; earliest-arrival ranging demands more than choosing the global maximum. Interference and sample-rate mismatch can also shift estimates.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 4: Frequency hopping and coexistence\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **A hopped carrier takes frequencies from an allowed set according to a time-indexed sequence; dwell duration, hop spacing and synchronization govern interference exposure.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: The gain disappears if an interferer follows the sequence or covers the full hopping band. Some coexistence systems need sensing and coordination rather than purely pseudo-random hopping.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 5: Joint design\n\nCombine two lessons in the multiuser field-monitoring network case. Propose a before/after experiment where only one controlled variable changes and show how you will report both a link-level metric and a delivered-payload metric.\n\n**Mark scheme:** prioritize controlled comparison (4), technically justified outcome (4), reproducible evidence (2).\n\n### Question 6: Critical evidence review\n\nFind a plausible but unsupported claim of improvement, identify which additional variable may account for the effect, and design one negative control to disprove your own conclusion.\n\n**Mark scheme:** claims supported by computed or experimentally generated data receive credit; appeals to authority or unsupported statements do not.\n\n## Submission policy\n\nUse explicit parameter values, code or workings, and a conclusion that distinguishes verified arithmetic from engineering assumptions.\n"
