          "heading": "Engineering Design Project: Linear Block Error-Correcting Codes: Generator and Parity-Check Matrices, Syndrome Decoding",
          "content": "# Unit 7 project \u2014 Optical fiber and photonic communication\n\n## Design brief\nCreate an original computer-based engineering investigation of regional optical transport route: two laboratory campuses share a long optical fiber cable. The stakeholder fears that loss, dispersion, front-end noise and wavelength filtering all enter design acceptance. Deliver a short technical report that combines the four models in Unit 7, an independent numeric calculation, a simple synthetic implementation and a comparison between two candidate solutions.\n\n## Required artifact A \u2014 model assumptions and operating points\n\nWrite a system block diagram and a table defining the reference points for all quantities in the four lessons. Include a benign operating point and two stresses, one concerning **dispersion, pulse spreading and the time budget** and the other concerning **wdm, coherent optics and nonlinear limits**. Give symbolic units and numerical values. Make clear which values are synthetic; do not label simulated observations as measured data.\n\n## Required artifact B \u2014 two methods, one controlled comparison\n\nImplement an equation or algorithm from **Fiber attenuation and optical power margin**, then independently recompute a selected point. Next vary an input related to **Photodiodes, responsivity and receiver noise** while holding the baseline model fixed. Record the dependent metric at at least ten settings; if the output is an estimated failure probability, run multiple seeds and show sampling uncertainty. Report a parameter setting at which the second candidate outperforms the first and one at which it does not.\n\n## Required artifact C \u2014 intentionally broken assumption\n\nThe limitations you must investigate include: (1) A sensitivity value must state bit rate, modulation, BER/FEC threshold and receiver architecture; otherwise comparing manufacturers or receiver designs is not meaningful. (2) Doubling signal current need not double electrical SNR when thermal and shot noise scale differently. APD gain is not unconditionally beneficial because excess noise grows with multiplication. Choose the more relevant limitation for regional optical transport route, inject an impairment that invalidates the original assumption, and explain in technical language why the ideal calculation no longer predicts the result. An analysis that merely says \u201cnoise got worse\u201d will not pass this requirement.\n\n## Required artifact D \u2014 user-level consequence\n\nThe application is two laboratory campuses share a long optical fiber cable. Define payload size, raw resource rate, protocol overhead, retransmission rule and the deadline for an accepted message. Trace low-level failure through framing and decoding into delivery success, delay and useful goodput. Prefer a table containing both one-attempt performance and end-to-end outcomes. Explain what real deployment measurements would be required.\n\n## Reproducibility and reporting rubric\n\nSubmit the source code, version or runtime description, seed list, parameter ranges and generated CSV. Use descriptive filenames, include a short README with instructions for a fresh run, and test one identity or limiting case. Cite the assumptions that make each formula valid. Allocate 30% to physics and mathematical correctness, 25% to experiment design, 20% to interpretation and failure analysis, 15% to reproducibility, and 10% to clarity. An unsupported claim of production readiness receives no credit.\n\n## Oral defense prompts\n\nExplain why **Dispersion, pulse spreading and the time budget** was included or excluded in the final design. Which numerical result is most sensitive to a poorly known parameter? What single new piece of hardware or field evidence would most change the conclusion? Finally, explain why the synthetic model is adequate for learning but does not establish a radio license, an optical product certification or safety compliance.\n"
