          "id": "sec-7-7",
          "secNumber": "7.7",
          "heading": "Analytical Derivations and Assessment: Linear Block Error-Correcting Codes: Generator and Parity-Check Matrices, Syndrome Decoding",
          "content": "# Unit 7 assessment \u2014 Optical fiber and photonic communication\n\n**Context:** two laboratory campuses share a long optical fiber cable\n\n## Problems (at least three required per unit)\n\n### Question 1: Fiber attenuation and optical power margin\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **A simple optical budget is P_rx(dBm)=P_tx(dBm)-\u03b1(dB/km)L(km)-L_connectors-L_splices-L_other, assuming no inline amplifiers.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: A sensitivity value must state bit rate, modulation, BER/FEC threshold and receiver architecture; otherwise comparing manufacturers or receiver designs is not meaningful.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 2: Dispersion, pulse spreading and the time budget\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **First-order chromatic spreading can be approximated by \u0394T\u2248|D| L \u0394\u03bb, where D is ps/(nm km), length L is km, and source spectral width \u0394\u03bb is nm.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: Dispersion parameter D may be near zero at one wavelength and substantial elsewhere. Do not apply a single attenuation coefficient and dispersion value across an entire band without verification.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 3: Photodiodes, responsivity and receiver noise\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **A photodiode's average signal current is I_s=R P_opt, where R is responsivity in A/W and P_opt is optical power in watts.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: Doubling signal current need not double electrical SNR when thermal and shot noise scale differently. APD gain is not unconditionally beneficial because excess noise grows with multiplication.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 4: WDM, coherent optics and nonlinear limits\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **For channel spacing \u0394f and center frequency f0=c/\u03bb0, a small wavelength spacing is approximately |\u0394\u03bb|\u2248(\u03bb0\u00b2/c)\u0394f.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: Adding more launch power can worsen nonlinear interference and reduce achievable information rate. Linear power-budget extrapolation is therefore unsafe for dense amplified systems.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 5: Joint design\n\nCombine two lessons in the regional optical transport route case. Propose a before/after experiment where only one controlled variable changes and show how you will report both a link-level metric and a delivered-payload metric.\n\n**Mark scheme:** prioritize controlled comparison (4), technically justified outcome (4), reproducible evidence (2).\n\n### Question 6: Critical evidence review\n\nFind a plausible but unsupported claim of improvement, identify which additional variable may account for the effect, and design one negative control to disprove your own conclusion.\n\n**Mark scheme:** claims supported by computed or experimentally generated data receive credit; appeals to authority or unsupported statements do not.\n\n## Submission policy\n\nUse explicit parameter values, code or workings, and a conclusion that distinguishes verified arithmetic from engineering assumptions.\n"
