          "heading": "Engineering Design Project: Convolutional Codes, Trellis Diagrams, Viterbi Decoding Algorithm, and Modern FEC",
          "content": "# Unit 8 project \u2014 Practical link and network engineering\n\n## Design brief\nCreate an original computer-based engineering investigation of emergency communications service: a regional network carries mixed urgent control traffic and bulk data. The stakeholder fears that latency tails and observed delivery reliability are contractual requirements. Deliver a short technical report that combines the four models in Unit 8, an independent numeric calculation, a simple synthetic implementation and a comparison between two candidate solutions.\n\n## Required artifact A \u2014 model assumptions and operating points\n\nWrite a system block diagram and a table defining the reference points for all quantities in the four lessons. Include a benign operating point and two stresses, one concerning **arq, hybrid arq and retransmission** and the other concerning **validation, reproducibility and deployment boundaries**. Give symbolic units and numerical values. Make clear which values are synthetic; do not label simulated observations as measured data.\n\n## Required artifact B \u2014 two methods, one controlled comparison\n\nImplement an equation or algorithm from **Adaptive modulation and coding**, then independently recompute a selected point. Next vary an input related to **Queueing, latency and quality of service** while holding the baseline model fixed. Record the dependent metric at at least ten settings; if the output is an estimated failure probability, run multiple seeds and show sampling uncertainty. Report a parameter setting at which the second candidate outperforms the first and one at which it does not.\n\n## Required artifact C \u2014 intentionally broken assumption\n\nThe limitations you must investigate include: (1) Goodput figures are dependent on traffic pattern, failure correlation, headers and retransmission details. A controller optimized using mean SNR can fail severely during rare fades. (2) Little's law is broadly valid for steady systems with consistent counting, but the exact M/M/1 mean waiting-time formula depends on its arrival and service assumptions. Report the 95th percentile rather than only mean latency for interactive services. Choose the more relevant limitation for emergency communications service, inject an impairment that invalidates the original assumption, and explain in technical language why the ideal calculation no longer predicts the result. An analysis that merely says \u201cnoise got worse\u201d will not pass this requirement.\n\n## Required artifact D \u2014 user-level consequence\n\nThe application is a regional network carries mixed urgent control traffic and bulk data. Define payload size, raw resource rate, protocol overhead, retransmission rule and the deadline for an accepted message. Trace low-level failure through framing and decoding into delivery success, delay and useful goodput. Prefer a table containing both one-attempt performance and end-to-end outcomes. Explain what real deployment measurements would be required.\n\n## Reproducibility and reporting rubric\n\nSubmit the source code, version or runtime description, seed list, parameter ranges and generated CSV. Use descriptive filenames, include a short README with instructions for a fresh run, and test one identity or limiting case. Cite the assumptions that make each formula valid. Allocate 30% to physics and mathematical correctness, 25% to experiment design, 20% to interpretation and failure analysis, 15% to reproducibility, and 10% to clarity. An unsupported claim of production readiness receives no credit.\n\n## Oral defense prompts\n\nExplain why **ARQ, hybrid ARQ and retransmission** was included or excluded in the final design. Which numerical result is most sensitive to a poorly known parameter? What single new piece of hardware or field evidence would most change the conclusion? Finally, explain why the synthetic model is adequate for learning but does not establish a radio license, an optical product certification or safety compliance.\n"
