          "heading": "Analytical Derivations and Assessment: Convolutional Codes, Trellis Diagrams, Viterbi Decoding Algorithm, and Modern FEC",
          "content": "# Unit 8 assessment \u2014 Practical link and network engineering\n\n**Context:** a regional network carries mixed urgent control traffic and bulk data\n\n## Problems (at least three required per unit)\n\n### Question 1: Adaptive modulation and coding\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **A link-adaptation controller selects a modulation/coding scheme using estimated channel quality, predicted packet error and implementation-specific throughput tables.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: Goodput figures are dependent on traffic pattern, failure correlation, headers and retransmission details. A controller optimized using mean SNR can fail severely during rare fades.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 2: ARQ, hybrid ARQ and retransmission\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **For independent packet attempts with success probability p, expected attempts to success are 1/p; HARQ may combine failed transmissions so attempts are not statistically identical.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: A geometric retry model is wrong when fading remains correlated across attempts or incremental redundancy changes conditional success probability after each transmission.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 3: Queueing, latency and quality of service\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **For a stable M/M/1 queue with arrival rate \u03bb and service rate \u03bc>\u03bb, mean system time is W=1/(\u03bc-\u03bb), and mean number in system L=\u03bbW.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: Little's law is broadly valid for steady systems with consistent counting, but the exact M/M/1 mean waiting-time formula depends on its arrival and service assumptions. Report the 95th percentile rather than only mean latency for interactive services.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 4: Validation, reproducibility and deployment boundaries\n\n**Problem:** Starting with **An end-to-end measured goodput G is accepted user payload divided by elapsed observation time; simulated symbol BER, packet error rate, EVM and goodput answer different questions.**, produce a dimensionally consistent worked example and one limiting case, then identify the violated assumption in this scenario: A visually attractive plot can hide a normalization bug; in simulation, independent recomputation of selected numerical points is essential. Correlation across trials invalidates common binomial uncertainty calculations.\n\n**Mark scheme (10 points):** 3 for model and assumptions, 3 for correct arithmetic or logical steps, 2 for limiting-case check, 2 for explaining why the stated limitation changes the conclusion. A correct final number without units and reference plane earns no more than 5 points.\n\n### Question 5: Joint design\n\nCombine two lessons in the emergency communications service case. Propose a before/after experiment where only one controlled variable changes and show how you will report both a link-level metric and a delivered-payload metric.\n\n**Mark scheme:** prioritize controlled comparison (4), technically justified outcome (4), reproducible evidence (2).\n\n### Question 6: Critical evidence review\n\nFind a plausible but unsupported claim of improvement, identify which additional variable may account for the effect, and design one negative control to disprove your own conclusion.\n\n**Mark scheme:** claims supported by computed or experimentally generated data receive credit; appeals to authority or unsupported statements do not.\n\n## Submission policy\n\nUse explicit parameter values, code or workings, and a conclusion that distinguishes verified arithmetic from engineering assumptions.\n"
